,"evidence":[{"claim":"Share of the grain crops' critical periods falling in months the model found costly","data_source_id":"D2;D1;S01","metric":"stress exposure (model-weighted)","value":0.581},{"claim":"Share of the year with bare soil","data_source_id":"S01","metric":"bare fraction","value":0.566},{"claim":"Bare soil during months that bring Emmet County's heavy-rain days","data_source_id":"D2","metric":"erosion exposure (share of heavy-rain days on bare soil)","value":0.276}],"benefits":["Familiar markets, equipment and management.","Lowest labour of the three in ISU budgets (about 2.2-2.6 operator hours/acre per year; S10)."],"information_gaps":["Field-level soil, drainage and slope: the tool uses county-scale data only.","Local markets and prices (see ISU Ag Decision Maker crop budgets, S10).","Your equipment, labour and custom-hire options.","Yield response of crops the model does not cover (oats, red clover, cereal rye)."],"risks_needing_local_review":["Soil is bare about 57% of the year, including months with heavy rain.","A two-crop cycle gives soybean cyst nematode a host every other year (S11)."],"modeled_years":2,"not_modeled_years":0,"cycle_years":2,"confidence_note":"2 of 2 rotation years are modelled; 8 of 20 trait values sourced, 20 not yet verified by a human. Scores are relative and come from a transparent formula, not a predicted yield.","trait_coverage":{"sourced":8,"total":20,"verified_by_human":0,"unverified":20},"sources":["D1","D2","S01","S10"],"support_line":"Supports your decision; does not replace your judgment or local expert advice."},"B":{"strategy_id":"B","name":"Corn \u2192 soybean + winter cereal rye","criteria":{"StressExposure":0.0,"BareAndErosion":1.0,"IncomeStability":0.0,"LaborBurden":0.0,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null},"excluded_criteria":["WaterUse: insufficient sourced data (2 of 5 trait values sourced)","SoilHealth: insufficient sourced data (6 of 20 trait values sourced)"],"why_suggested":"The field is bare about 57% of the year under corn-soybean. Cereal rye seeded right after harvest (S05) covers the soil through winter and spring, when much of the county's heavy rain falls.","evidence":[{"claim":"Share of the grain crops' critical periods falling in months the model found costly","data_source_id":"D2;D1;S01","metric":"stress exposure (model-weighted)","value":0.581},{"claim":"Share of the year with bare soil","data_source_id":"S01;S04;S05","metric":"bare fraction","value":0.018},{"claim":"Bare soil during months that bring Emmet County's heavy-rain days","data_source_id":"D2","metric":"erosion exposure (share of heavy-rain days on bare soil)","value":0.024}],"benefits":["Bare-soil share falls from 57% to 2% of the year.","Rye produces 3,000-10,000 lb/acre of dry matter (SARE, S06) and holds soil and nutrients (S05).","Same cash crops, so no new grain markets are needed."],"information_gaps":["Field-level soil, drainage and slope: the tool uses county-scale data only.","Local markets and prices (see ISU Ag Decision Maker crop budgets, S10).","Your equipment, labour and custom-hire options.","Yield response of crops the model does not cover (oats, red clover, cereal rye)."],"risks_needing_local_review":["Rye decomposition ties up soil nitrogen for the next corn crop, more if rye has started jointing; terminate at least 10-14 days before corn planting (ISU, S04).","Possible corn yield drag after rye; Iowa survey data show smaller drag for experienced users (S12).","Seeding right after harvest and spring termination timing both depend on the season (S04; S05).","Without cost-share or grazing, cover crops returned about -$38 to -$49 per acre in an Iowa survey (S12); check local cost-share programs."],"modeled_years":2,"not_modeled_years":0,"cycle_years":2,"confidence_note":"2 of 2 rotation years are modelled; 13 of 30 trait values sourced, 30 not yet verified by a human. Scores are relative and come from a transparent formula, not a predicted yield.","trait_coverage":{"sourced":13,"total":30,"verified_by_human":0,"unverified":30},"sources":["D1","D2","S01","S04","S05","S06","S10","S12"],"support_line":"Supports your decision; does not replace your judgment or local expert advice."},"C":{"strategy_id":"C","name":"Corn \u2192 soybean \u2192 oats with red clover","criteria":{"StressExposure":1.0,"BareAndErosion":0.446,"IncomeStability":1.0,"LaborBurden":0.216,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null},"excluded_criteria":["WaterUse: insufficient sourced data (2 of 5 trait values sourced)","SoilHealth: insufficient sourced data (6 of 20 trait values sourced)"],"why_suggested":"The model puts 65% of corn's weather sensitivity in July. The oat crop's critical period (heading to colouring) ends around July 9 in a typical Iowa year (NASS Crop Progress, S01), so one year in three has a grain crop that is mostly past its sensitive stage before the peak stress window. This is calendar exposure, not a predicted yield.","evidence":[{"claim":"Share of the grain crops' critical periods falling in months the model found costly","data_source_id":"D2;D1;S01","metric":"stress exposure (model-weighted)","value":0.496},{"claim":"Share of the year with bare soil","data_source_id":"S01;S07;S08","metric":"bare fraction","value":0.342},{"claim":"Bare soil during months that bring Emmet County's heavy-rain days","data_source_id":"D2","metric":"erosion exposure (share of heavy-rain days on bare soil)","value":0.154}],"benefits":["At Marsden Farm in neighbouring Boone County, the 3-year rotation had corn yields ~4% and soybean yields ~9% higher than corn-soybean, with ~80% less synthetic N and 88% less herbicide (S09).","Net returns were similar to corn-soybean and varied less from year to year (S09).","Red clover supplies 70-150 lb N/acre and excellent forage (S06; S07)."],"information_gaps":["Field-level soil, drainage and slope: the tool uses county-scale data only.","Local markets and prices (see ISU Ag Decision Maker crop budgets, S10).","Your equipment, labour and custom-hire options.","Yield response of crops the model does not cover (oats, red clover, cereal rye)."],"risks_needing_local_review":["Needs small-grain harvest and hay or grazing equipment, and more labour (>33% more at Marsden; S09).","Local markets for oats, straw and hay were not checked.","Red clover establishment can fail in a dry or very wet spring (S07; S08).","Clovers are soybean cyst nematode hosts (ISU, S11): review with your agronomist.","Marsden Farm also used composted manure; results may not transfer directly."],"modeled_years":2,"not_modeled_years":1,"cycle_years":3,"confidence_note":"2 of 3 rotation years are modelled; 17 of 40 trait values sourced, 40 not yet verified by a human. Scores are relative and come from a transparent formula, not a predicted yield.","trait_coverage":{"sourced":17,"total":40,"verified_by_human":0,"unverified":40},"sources":["D1","D2","S01","S06","S07","S08","S09","S10","S11"],"support_line":"Supports your decision; does not replace your judgment or local expert advice."}},"calendar_shifts":{"-21":{"criteria":{"A":{"StressExposure":0.0,"BareAndErosion":0.0,"IncomeStability":0.9776,"LaborBurden":1.0,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null},"B":{"StressExposure":0.0,"BareAndErosion":1.0,"IncomeStability":0.0,"LaborBurden":0.0,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null},"C":{"StressExposure":1.0,"BareAndErosion":0.4256,"IncomeStability":1.0,"LaborBurden":0.2162,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null}},"stress_exposure":{"A":0.7366,"B":0.7366,"C":0.5286}},"-14":{"criteria":{"A":{"StressExposure":0.0,"BareAndErosion":0.0,"IncomeStability":0.9776,"LaborBurden":1.0,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null},"B":{"StressExposure":0.0,"BareAndErosion":1.0,"IncomeStability":0.0,"LaborBurden":0.0,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null},"C":{"StressExposure":1.0,"BareAndErosion":0.438,"IncomeStability":1.0,"LaborBurden":0.2162,"WaterUse":null,"SoilHealth":null}},"stress_exposure":{"A":0.7526,"B":0.7526,"C":0.5538}},"0":{"criteria":{"A":{"StressExposure":0.0,"BareAndErosion